{
  "dom_id": "step-q125496",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14221961/assets/a5f23af5dd514a65937eee29bb0992ff94d68897d8c6422fcf215aad45379c4a.png)\n\nConsider the part of parabola and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the part of parabola is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works with the particle for $y<0,$ while $F$  works against the particle for $y>0.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14221961/assets/537aca6190fc2f3e06e85c0781a8bb461559ef0a7515ee29aca505f9d615f820.png)\n\n \n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $y<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $y>0.$ So,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14221961/assets/4939f9a22400a7a56eaeacb77d520e522f50aa8378ca1014a19810122e12da51.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
